1: \documentclass[11pt]{article}
2: \usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
3: \usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,longtable,booktabs}
4: \usepackage[colorlinks=true,allcolors=blue]{hyperref}
5: \title{Explicit graph invariants and exact Jacobian certificates\\
6: for a self-dual five-form in ten dimensions}
7: \author{\texttt{uskutsav-cpu}}
8: \date{September 7, 2026}
9: \begin{document}
10: \maketitle
11: \begin{abstract}
12: We give a reproducible certificate for 81 explicit contractions of a real
13: self-dual five-form in signature $(1,9)$. The fixed selection consists of one
14: quartic, two sextic, six octic, twelve degree-ten, and sixty degree-twelve
15: polynomials. Their Jacobian has a nonzero $81\times81$ minor at four exact
16: finite-field specializations, using the same ordered functions in every
17: case. An integral coordinate convention makes each nonzero minor a
18: characteristic-zero independence witness when its entries are verified as
19: derivatives of the specified contractions. Together with the generic quotient
20: dimension from the literature, this gives a maximal generically independent
21: family. It does not determine all polynomial generators or syzygies.
22: \end{abstract}
23: 
24: \section{Scope and conventions}
25: Let $F$ be an alternating five-form, with metric
26: $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,\ldots,1)$ and Hodge convention
27: $\star F=F$. The self-dual subspace has dimension 126.
28: The literature supplies the generic quotient dimension 81 and the
29: low-degree Hilbert coefficients \cite{cederwall,hutomo}. The present
30: certificate supplies the matching lower bound using explicit graph
31: contractions. The contraction catalogs and previously discovered bases are
32: inherited from the repository's research branches; the certificate reported
33: here re-evaluates those functions at new points.
34: Graph enumeration and tensor-network contraction followed by numerical linear
35: algebra are established methods for tensor invariants: see
36: Elamaran, Ferko and Scarlett \cite{elamaran}, whose six-dimensional three-form
37: study leaves the ten-dimensional chiral five-form application for future work.
38: We make no claim of priority for this method or for an 81-invariant family.
39: 
40: A graph has one vertex per copy of $F$, no loops, and valence five.
41: An edge of multiplicity $m$ contracts $m$ index pairs with inverse metrics.
42: Vertices and edges are ordered; slot order is fixed by lexicographic edge
43: order. Graph isomorphism alone does not fix the sign of an odd-form
44: contraction, so the formula identifier hashes the ordered edge record.
45: Disconnected contractions are products. The quadratic contraction vanishes
46: on this self-dual space: $F\wedge\star F=F\wedge F=0$, since $F$ has odd
47: degree, so its metric norm vanishes. Complete contraction with the invariant
48: metric proves Lorentz invariance of each graph polynomial.
49: 
50: For each increasing five-tuple $I$ containing 0, define
51: $A_I=F_I$ and let $e_I$ denote its compact alternating component vector.
52: The integral parametrization is
53: \[
54:  F(A)=\sum_{I\ni0} A_I(e_I+\star e_I).
55: \]
56: The 126 columns contain only $0,1,-1$; their components indexed by $I\ni0$
57: form an identity matrix. Every graph polynomial therefore has integer
58: coefficients in $A$, with no projector denominators.
59: 
60: \section{Fixed selection and exact witness}
61: The nine low-degree functions are those in
62: \path{results/10d_order8.json}; the next twelve come from
63: \path{results/10d_order10.json}. The first sixty selected functional
64: directions in \path{results/10d_order12.json} complete the list.
65: The inherited inventory labels two further degree-twelve directions as
66: primitive polynomial directions; they are not included in the 81-function
67: Jacobian. That label requires the separate homogeneous-space evidence below. A dependent Jacobian row does not identify a polynomial syzygy.
68: 
69: \begin{center}
70: \begin{tabular}{rrr}
71: \toprule
72: Field degree & New functional directions & Cumulative rank\\
73: \midrule
74: 4 & 1 & 1\\
75: 6 & 2 & 3\\
76: 8 & 6 & 9\\
77: 10 & 12 & 21\\
78: 12 & 60 & 81\\
79: \bottomrule
80: \end{tabular}
81: \end{center}
82: 
83: Write $J_{aI}=\partial G_a/\partial A_I$. Each certificate cell records
84: the prime, all 126 coordinates, all 252 self-dual compact components,
85: the same ordered 81 graph identifiers, all entries of the $81\times126$
86: Jacobian, 81 pivot columns, and the determinant of that minor.
87: \input{tables/certificate_cells.tex}
88: The determinant is recomputed by Python-integer elimination independently
89: of the streaming rank sieve. Euler's identity
90: $\sum_I A_I J_{aI}=\deg(G_a)G_a$ is checked for every row.
91: 
92: Suppose the recorded entries have been verified as the Jacobian of the
93: specified integral graph polynomials at the recorded point. A nonzero minor
94: modulo $p$ then proves that the corresponding integer determinant polynomial
95: is not identically zero. In characteristic zero the Jacobian criterion gives
96: algebraic independence of these 81 polynomials, and their differentials are
97: independent on the nonempty Zariski-open locus where that minor is nonzero.
98: Invariance and the cited generic quotient dimension supply the matching upper
99: bound. On a regular local quotient this gives local coordinates. It proves
100: neither global orbit separation, generation of the invariant field by rational
101: functions in this list, nor polynomial generation of the entire ring.
102: 
103: \section{Polynomial spaces and literature comparison}
104: The homogeneous-space dimensions reported in equation (4.2) of
105: \cite{cederwall} through degree twelve are $1,2,7,14,72$.
106: These representation-theoretic counts are an external input here. Products must be included in value-space comparisons.
107: At degree twelve the ten product monomials are $I_4^3$, the three symmetric
108: products of sextics, and the six products $I_4 I_{8,j}$. The inherited
109: degree-twelve computation reaches rank 72 using these products and 62 graph
110: directions. A rank-72 concatenated evaluation proves independence of that
111: homogeneous list only after the evaluated polynomial rows are verified; the
112: matching Hilbert coefficient supplies completeness. This inherited computation
113: is separate from the 81-function certificate. Polynomial-space evidence uses
114: gradients concatenated at several points; the functional Jacobian uses one
115: point. These are separate sieves. The prose of section 4.1.4 of
116: \cite{cederwall} says 64 new degree-twelve directions, inconsistent with its
117: equation (4.2), which has exponent 62. The inventory here uses the latter;
118: we do not treat the inconsistent prose as an additional verified count.
119: Factor exponents in a Hilbert product are not automatically generator counts
120: at higher degrees when generators and relations may coexist.
121: 
122: The octic comparison uses a selection of six displayed literature
123: contractions: the first five expressions from equations (4.12)--(4.15) of
124: \cite{cederwall}, together with the hatted expression (4.18). The
125: machine-readable matrix has exact reconstructed rational entries and includes
126: the quartic-square product explicitly. Its interpretation as a polynomial
127: change of basis is supported by modular fits and fresh value holdouts;
128: these finite checks are not a proof of every proposed polynomial identity.
129: The $6\times6$ block compares primitive classes modulo products; any product
130: correction is retained in the full $7\times7$ matrix.
131: 
132: The archived degree-ten map concerns the repository's implemented readings,
133: whose reconstructed $12\times14$ coordinate matrix has rank twelve and
134: intersection dimension one with the two-dimensional product subspace.
135: A source audit found that the antisymmetrizers in the archived implementations
136: of candidates $J_{10}$--$J_{12}$ differ from the primary PDF and TeX source.
137: Consequently these matrix ranks cannot be attributed to the twelve published
138: expressions. Any repaired source map must be identified by its own formula
139: registry and fresh evaluation artifacts; the archived atlas is not silently
140: reinterpreted as that map. The archived degree-ten regeneration reads stored
141: per-prime coordinates and checks rational reconstruction against a held-out
142: stored prime; it performs no fresh tensor evaluations. Even fresh finite
143: holdouts would not by themselves prove characteristic-zero polynomial
144: identities. This source-transcription failure concerns the literature map;
145: the graph-only nonzero-minor argument has separate inputs.
146: 
147: \section{Reproduction and validation}
148: Use the declared dependencies and run the regression suite before evaluating
149: certificates. The original low-degree commit is preserved by
150: \texttt{v0.1-low-degree-complete}. The release commands are:
151: \begin{verbatim}
152: python scripts/graph_to_latex.py
153: python scripts/search_rank81.py
154: python scripts/verify_rank81.py
155: python scripts/verify_rank81.py --recompute
156: python scripts/validate_rank81_lorentz.py
157: python scripts/map_literature_basis.py --degree 8
158: python scripts/map_literature_basis.py --degree 10
159: \end{verbatim}
160: The default verifier checks the saved matrix and metadata; the explicit
161: \texttt{--recompute} mode repeats the graph evaluations with the production
162: evaluator. This recomputation does not by itself constitute an independent
163: implementation of the derivative. These modes are reported separately. The search stage checkpoints each raw row and its
164: pivot, checks run identity on resume, and bounds worker submissions.
165: Degree-specific discovery remains available through the existing nauty
166: pipelines. \texttt{run\_degree.py} accepts additional edge-list JSONL streams
167: and separates polynomial completeness from functional rank.
168: 
169: Regression tests cover the 6D reproduction, self-duality,
170: the zero quadratic contraction, exact integer arithmetic, reverse
171: Jacobians against an independent oracle, graph parsing beyond ten vertices,
172: and genuine Lorentz boosts. The final graph selection is checked under both
173: a spatial rotation and a boost at the recorded finite-field point; neither uses
174: floating-point tolerances. These are implementation checks at specified inputs,
175: while invariance itself follows from the complete-contraction definition.
176: 
